title: The Call for Proposals Is Open
---
author: ORGANIZERS
---
pub_date: 2026-11-04
---
body:

The [Call for Proposals](/call-for-proposals/) for PyCon DE 2027 is open. We want to hear what you've built, broken, learned and figured out, and we want you on stage in Heidelberg.

**Deadline: 20 December 2026, 23:59:59 CET.**

## Five tracks

Pick a track and a topic for your proposal:

- **Core Python & Web:** Python Language & Ecosystem; Software Engineering, Testing & Architecture; Django, Web Frameworks & APIs; Embedded Systems, Hardware & Robotics
- **Applied AI & Data Engineering:** Data Pipelines, Storage & Architecture; Machine Learning, Deep Learning & Predictive AI; Generative AI, LLMs & Audio/NLP; Computer Vision & Spatial Computing; AI-Assisted Software Engineering
- **Operations, Cloud & Systems:** MLOps, Cloud Infrastructure & DevOps; Autonomous Systems & AI Agents; Systems Programming & Cross-Language Interoperability; Security, DevSecOps & Privacy
- **Organizational Practice & Future Work:** Ethics, Governance & Responsible AI; Education, Career Development & Mentorship; AI Transformation
- **Community:** Community & Community Building; Diversity & Inclusion; Culture

Talks run 30 or 45 minutes, hands-on tutorials 90 minutes. Our audience is experienced: in last year's self-evaluation, 80% of attendees rated themselves intermediate or advanced. Go deep. For inspiration, watch past talks on [PyConDE](https://www.youtube.com/pyconde) and [PyData](https://www.youtube.com/user/PyDataTV/) on YouTube.

## What makes a strong proposal

Your abstract is published as is in the conference program, and attendees read it closely when choosing talks. Make sure it answers four questions: What problem does your talk address? Why does it matter to the audience? What is your solution? What will people take home? For tutorials, tell us exactly what participants will have learned at the end.

A few more things we look for:

- **New material.** Talks already given at other conferences or events are rejected automatically.
- **Open source.** If your work uses open-source tools, name the libraries. Ideally, the audience can reproduce your results with open code and data.
- **Practice welcome, sales pitches not.** We love talks about applying tools and concepts at work or in your free time, and discourage proposals whose sole aim is to sell a product.
- **Up to three talks per organization**, so the program stays diverse. Want your company to have a deeper impact? Become a [sponsor](/sponsoring/).

After the deadline, proposals are peer-reviewed and then selected by the program committee. Speakers present on site in Heidelberg, and each accepted submission receives one free ticket. Already [bought a ticket](/buy-ticket/)? We refund it, and you keep your edition and shirt.

## First time on stage?

We especially encourage first-time speakers and submissions by underrepresented members of the community. We can't cover speakers' travel by default, but our [Financial Aid Programme](/financial-aid/) is open to everyone. If you need support to travel to Heidelberg, apply right after you submit. Don't wait for the acceptance notification.

All speakers and attendees follow our [Code of Conduct](/code-of-conduct/).

## Is the CfP the only way onto the stage?

For the program, yes: every talk goes through the Call for Proposals and peer review. The one exception is sponsored talks. A limited number of [sponsor packages](/sponsoring/) include a talk slot, also subject to approval by the program committee. These slots can only be booked until **3 February 2027**.

## Key dates

- **20 December 2026:** CfP closes
- **10 February 2027:** Acceptance notifications
- **19 April 2027:** [Masterclasses](/masterclasses/) and [Django Girls](/satellite-events/)
- **20–22 April 2027:** Conference
- **23–24 April 2027:** [Sprints](/sprints/), [hackathons](/satellite-events/) and Beginners' Day

## More ways to contribute

Not a talk, but a full day of teaching? Answer the [Call for Masterclasses](/masterclasses/call/). Maintaining an open-source project? [Propose a sprint](/sprints/). Planning a hackathon or meetup? See [satellite events](/satellite-events/).

[Read the full Call for Proposals](/call-for-proposals/) or [submit your proposal on Pretalx](https://pretalx.com/pyconde-2027/cfp). You can edit your proposal until the CfP closes.

Know someone with a great story to tell? Forward this post.

— The PyCon DE Team
